1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,505
What is a remote?

2
00:00:07,067 --> 00:00:13,293
A remote is another copy of the repository that yours knows by a short name, usually origin.

3
00:00:13,293 --> 00:00:22,701
We look at how your clone remembers the remote's branches (origin/main) and why they are only a snapshot of the last time you talked to it.

4
00:00:24,467 --> 00:00:28,866
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

5
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:36,778
origin is the name, the path is the address (on GitHub it is https://github.com/...

6
00:00:36,778 --> 00:00:38,822
or [email]:...).

7
00:00:38,822 --> 00:00:44,244
One for fetching, one for pushing; they are usually the same.

8
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:52,050
origin is the name, the path is the address (on GitHub it is https://github.com/...

9
00:00:52,050 --> 00:00:54,059
or [email]:...).

10
00:00:54,059 --> 00:00:59,387
One for fetching, one for pushing; they are usually the same.

11
00:00:59,387 --> 00:01:05,588
origin/main (shown as remotes/origin/main) is a remote-tracking branch:

12
00:01:05,588 --> 00:01:09,169
your clone's memory of the server's main.

13
00:01:10,933 --> 00:01:12,885
Let me try something.

14
00:01:12,885 --> 00:01:15,208
Grace commits and pushes.

15
00:01:15,767 --> 00:01:18,835
Ada's origin/main still shows Add prices.

16
00:01:18,835 --> 00:01:21,679
Remote-tracking branches are not live:

17
00:01:21,679 --> 00:01:25,946
they are updated only by git fetch, git pull or git push.

18
00:01:25,946 --> 00:01:30,436
Ada's clone has not talked to the server since Grace pushed.

19
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:33,999
Here is the fix.

20
00:01:35,733 --> 00:01:36,447
Got it.

21
00:01:36,447 --> 00:01:39,198
What do I need to remember?

22
00:01:39,733 --> 00:01:45,698
A remote is a named URL of another copy; origin is the default name after cloning.

23
00:01:46,233 --> 00:01:54,773
origin/main is a local, read-only snapshot of the server's main, updated only by fetch/pull/push.

24
00:01:55,333 --> 00:01:58,368
git ls-remote asks the server live.

25
00:02:00,133 --> 00:02:03,041
I keep hearing about git remote.

26
00:02:03,041 --> 00:02:04,768
What is it, really?

27
00:02:05,333 --> 00:02:06,863
Managing remotes:

28
00:02:06,863 --> 00:02:10,101
add, rename, change the URL, remove.

29
00:02:10,101 --> 00:02:16,848
And working with two remotes, the common origin + upstream setup of a fork.

