46
00:03:18,280 --> 00:03:21,031
What do I need to remember?

47
00:03:21,567 --> 00:03:27,381
git remote add / set-url / rename / remove manage the list of remotes.

48
00:03:27,933 --> 00:03:32,508
One origin; change its URL instead of adding it again.

49
00:03:33,067 --> 00:03:38,931
Forks usually have origin (your fork) and upstream (the original).

50
00:03:40,667 --> 00:03:42,911
How does git clone work?

51
00:03:43,467 --> 00:03:51,816
git clone URL copies a whole repository (every commit, every branch) and sets up origin and the local main.

52
00:03:51,816 --> 00:03:57,746
We also see shallow clones and cloning a specific branch, both common in CI.

53
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:03,899
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

54
00:04:04,433 --> 00:04:08,460
Only main is a local branch, but every commit is there:

55
00:04:08,460 --> 00:04:11,461
feature-tea exists as origin/feature-tea.

56
00:04:11,461 --> 00:04:15,488
Switching to it creates the local branch automatically:

57
00:04:16,033 --> 00:04:20,060
Only main is a local branch, but every commit is there:

58
00:04:20,060 --> 00:04:23,061
feature-tea exists as origin/feature-tea.

59
00:04:23,061 --> 00:04:27,088
Switching to it creates the local branch automatically:

60
00:04:28,833 --> 00:04:30,559
Let me try something.

61
00:04:30,559 --> 00:04:34,093
Clone into a folder that already has files:

62
00:04:34,633 --> 00:04:39,449
destination path 'ada' already exists and is not an empty directory.:

63
00:04:39,449 --> 00:04:42,032
Git never clones over existing files.

64
00:04:42,032 --> 00:04:47,127
Either the repository is already there (then you need git pull inside it,

65
00:04:47,127 --> 00:04:50,128
not a new clone), or choose another folder.

66
00:04:51,867 --> 00:04:53,666
Here is the fix.

67
00:04:55,400 --> 00:04:56,113
Got it.

68
00:04:56,113 --> 00:04:58,865
What do I need to remember?

69
00:04:59,400 --> 00:05:05,515
git clone = init + add origin + fetch everything + check out the default branch.

70
00:05:06,067 --> 00:05:12,526
Remote branches arrive as origin/*; git switch NAME creates the local tracking branch.

71
00:05:13,067 --> 00:05:18,091
--depth 1 for fast CI clones; --unshallow to get the history later.

72
00:05:19,833 --> 00:05:22,038
Why would I need git fetch?

73
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:29,867
git fetch downloads new commits from the remote and updates origin/*, without touching your branches or files.

74
00:05:29,867 --> 00:05:34,029
It is the safe way to see what changed before you integrate it.

75
00:05:35,767 --> 00:05:40,166
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

76
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:42,300
Let's run it.

77
00:05:42,833 --> 00:05:48,246
main..origin/main = "commits on origin/main that are not on main":

78
00:05:48,246 --> 00:05:51,116
exactly what a pull would bring in.

79
00:05:51,116 --> 00:05:53,658
Review them before integrating:

80
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:59,612
main..origin/main = "commits on origin/main that are not on main":

81
00:05:59,612 --> 00:06:02,482
exactly what a pull would bring in.

82
00:06:02,482 --> 00:06:05,024
Review them before integrating:

83
00:06:06,767 --> 00:06:08,363
Let me try something.

84
00:06:08,363 --> 00:06:13,456
Grace deletes a branch on the server; Ada's clone keeps showing it:

85
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:15,939
origin/old-promo is stale:

86
00:06:15,939 --> 00:06:21,829
a plain fetch adds and updates remote-tracking branches but never deletes them.

87
00:06:21,829 --> 00:06:26,900
On busy repositories this produces dozens of dead origin/* branches.

88
00:06:28,633 --> 00:06:30,091
Make it permanent:

89
00:06:30,091 --> 00:06:33,088
git config --global fetch.prune true.

90
00:06:34,833 --> 00:06:35,547
Got it.

91
00:06:35,547 --> 00:06:38,298
What do I need to remember?

92
00:06:38,833 --> 00:06:44,563
git fetch updates origin/* only; your branches and files are untouched.

93
00:06:45,100 --> 00:06:51,415
main..origin/main incoming, origin/main..main outgoing.

94
00:06:51,967 --> 00:06:57,526
--prune (or fetch.prune true) removes branches deleted on the server.

95
00:06:59,267 --> 00:07:01,791
What should I know about git pull?

96
00:07:02,333 --> 00:07:09,307
git pull = git fetch + integrate (merge or rebase) origin/<branch> into the current branch.

97
00:07:09,307 --> 00:07:16,818
We see the easy case, the "divergent branches" error, and how to configure the behaviour you want.

98
00:07:18,567 --> 00:07:22,966
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

99
00:07:23,500 --> 00:07:25,553
Ada has no local commits:

100
00:07:25,553 --> 00:07:27,770
the pull is a fast-forward.

101
00:07:29,533 --> 00:07:31,409
Let me try something.

102
00:07:31,409 --> 00:07:33,016
Both sides commit:

103
00:07:33,016 --> 00:07:35,963
Ada locally, Grace on the server.

104
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:36,921
fatal:

105
00:07:36,921 --> 00:07:40,638
Need to specify how to reconcile divergent branches.:

106
00:07:40,638 --> 00:07:42,953
Git fetched Grace's commit (look:

107
00:07:42,953 --> 00:07:48,424
origin/main moved) but refuses to choose between a merge and a rebase for you,

108
00:07:48,424 --> 00:07:51,300
because they produce different histories.

109
00:07:53,033 --> 00:07:53,576
Choose.

110
00:07:53,576 --> 00:07:58,463
The two options produce the same files but a different history:

111
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:00,913
Got it.

112
00:08:00,913 --> 00:08:03,665
What do I need to remember?

113
00:08:04,200 --> 00:08:07,630
git pull = fetch + merge or rebase.

114
00:08:08,167 --> 00:08:10,983
Diverged branches need a decision:

115
00:08:10,983 --> 00:08:15,621
--no-rebase (merge commit) or --rebase (linear history).

116
00:08:16,167 --> 00:08:22,741
Configure pull.rebase / pull.ff once; use --ff-only on shared branches.

117
00:08:24,500 --> 00:08:26,785
Can you show me git push?

118
00:08:27,333 --> 00:08:31,950
git push uploads your commits and moves the branch on the server.

119
00:08:31,950 --> 00:08:37,632
It only works as a fast-forward; when someone pushed before you, it is rejected.

120
00:08:37,632 --> 00:08:42,888
We see why, the right fix, and the safe way to force-push your own branch.

121
00:08:44,633 --> 00:08:49,033
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

122
00:08:49,567 --> 00:08:52,634
4267004..xxxxxxx main -> main:

123
00:08:52,634 --> 00:08:58,666
the server's main moved from the old commit to the new one.

124
00:08:58,666 --> 00:09:00,506
Push a new branch:

125
00:09:01,067 --> 00:09:04,134
4267004..xxxxxxx main -> main:

126
00:09:04,134 --> 00:09:10,166
the server's main moved from the old commit to the new one.

127
00:09:10,166 --> 00:09:12,006
Push a new branch:

128
00:09:13,767 --> 00:09:15,866
Let me try something.

129
00:09:15,866 --> 00:09:18,766
Grace pushes first; then Ada:

130
00:09:19,300 --> 00:09:19,364
!

131
00:09:19,364 --> 00:09:21,803
[rejected] main -> main (fetch first):

132
00:09:21,803 --> 00:09:25,910
the server's main has a commit (Grace's) that Ada does not have.

133
00:09:25,910 --> 00:09:33,161
A push must be a fast-forward of the server's branch; accepting Ada's push would delete Grace's commit from main.

134
00:09:33,161 --> 00:09:39,899
(non-fast-forward is the same rejection when Git already knows the remote commit from an earlier fetch.).

135
00:09:41,633 --> 00:09:44,755
Integrate first, then push.

136
00:09:44,755 --> 00:09:46,143
Not --force.

137
00:09:47,900 --> 00:09:48,613
Got it.

138
00:09:48,613 --> 00:09:51,364
What do I need to remember?

139
00:09:51,900 --> 00:09:56,000
A push must fast-forward the server's branch; otherwise it is rejected.

140
00:09:57,533 --> 00:10:02,928
Fix a rejection with git pull (merge or rebase), then push again.

141
00:10:03,467 --> 00:10:08,436
Only rewrite pushed history on your own branch, with --force-with-lease.

142
00:10:10,200 --> 00:10:13,626
I keep hearing about upstream branches.

143
00:10:13,626 --> 00:10:15,294
What is it, really?

144
00:10:15,833 --> 00:10:22,053
The upstream (tracking) branch is the remote branch a local branch is connected to.

145
00:10:22,053 --> 00:10:28,948
It is what lets you type plain git push, git pull and see "ahead 1, behind 2" in git status.

146
00:10:30,700 --> 00:10:35,099
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

147
00:10:35,633 --> 00:10:37,997
A new branch has no upstream yet.

148
00:10:37,997 --> 00:10:42,223
Push it with -u to create the remote branch and connect it:

149
00:10:42,767 --> 00:10:45,062
A new branch has no upstream yet.

150
00:10:45,062 --> 00:10:49,165
Push it with -u to create the remote branch and connect it:

151
00:10:49,165 --> 00:10:54,311
Now plain git push / git pull work on it, and git status compares with it:

152
00:10:54,867 --> 00:11:00,311
Now plain git push / git pull work on it, and git status compares with it:

153
00:11:02,067 --> 00:11:03,716
Let me try something.

154
00:11:03,716 --> 00:11:07,251
Create another branch and push it without -u:

155
00:11:07,800 --> 00:11:08,187
fatal:

156
00:11:08,187 --> 00:11:11,862
The current branch feature-mocha has no upstream branch.:

157
00:11:11,862 --> 00:11:18,117
Git does not know where to push this branch; it does not assume a remote branch of the same name.

158
00:11:18,117 --> 00:11:20,954
The message prints the exact command to use.

159
00:11:22,700 --> 00:11:24,960
To never see the error again:

160
00:11:24,960 --> 00:11:28,544
git config --global push.autoSetupRemote true.

161
00:11:30,300 --> 00:11:31,013
Got it.

162
00:11:31,013 --> 00:11:33,764
What do I need to remember?

163
00:11:34,300 --> 00:11:38,535
The upstream connects a local branch to a remote branch:

164
00:11:38,535 --> 00:11:41,559
plain push/pull and ahead/behind use it.

165
00:11:42,100 --> 00:11:47,844
git push -u origin BRANCH the first time (or push.autoSetupRemote true).

166
00:11:48,400 --> 00:11:52,439
Check with git branch -vv; fix with git branch -u.
